  "text": "The reality-virtuality continuum is introduced as a scale from a fully real environment to a fully virtual one. Augmented reality and augmented virtuality are opposite intermediate states on that scale, each involving one environment augmented with content from the other. Mixed reality is then described more broadly as the entire space between the two extremes, with different degrees of augmentation."
}

{
  "points": [
    {
      "facts": [1, 2],
      "point": "Extended Reality (XR) is an umbrella term whose X variable covers present and future immersive realities like AR, VR, MR."
    },
    {
      "facts": [3],
      "point": "A technology is immersive when its sensed experience appears real and cannot be distinguished from the person's real environment."
    }
  ],
  "text": "XR is a broad label, not a single technology, and the X in it acts like a variable for different immersive realities. AR, VR, and MR are among those realities, alongside possible future ones. The technologies covered by XR are defined by the experience they produce: a sensed environment that appears real and cannot be distinguished from the person's real environment."
}

{
  "points": [
    {
      "facts": [1, 2],
      "point": "Tracking is an essential XR hardware component because systems must track physical-world motion, especially in VR."
    },
    {
      "facts": [3],
      "point": "In VR, tracking translates the user's physical motion or input into motion in the virtual world."
    },
    {
      "facts": [4],
      "point": "In AR, tracking registers virtual 3D content to a real-world anchor and tracks user pose and moving objects in view."
    },
    {
      "facts": [5],
      "point": "Tracking is also called sensing, but the hardware devices are more often called sensors than trackers."
    }
  ],
  "text": "Tracking is a core hardware function in XR because a system must follow motion in the physical world. VR uses that tracking to turn the user's physical motion or input into virtual motion, while AR uses it to register virtual 3D content to a real-world anchor and follow the user's pose and moving objects. Tracking is also called sensing, although the enabling devices are usually called sensors."
}

{
